# Ambient capability acceptance unit (plan §7.4).
#
# The §7.4 example in one unit: drop to `nobody`, keep exactly one capability,
# and deny privilege escalation - the least-privilege shape the section is for.
#
# It is the case the bounding set alone cannot express. A non-root process gets
# no capabilities at `exec` unless they are *ambient*: the kernel clears
# permitted first thing (`get_file_caps`) and rebuilds it from file
# capabilities, the ambient set, and - for root - the bounding set. So the only
# way `nobody` ends up able to `bind()` a privileged port is an ambient
# capability, which is why `NET_BIND_SERVICE` is expected in `amb`, `eff` and
# `bnd` alike: `bnd` from `CapabilityBoundingSet=` (what it may ever hold), the
# other two raised by `AmbientCapabilities=`.
#
# `NoNewPrivileges=true` is part of the same shape and is checked here too: it
# denies escalation to children without taking away what the service was
# explicitly granted. Note that it also forbids every other path to privilege -
# a unit that needs a setuid helper cannot ask for this.
#
# Note `bin/cut`: the applet is at /usr/bin/cut in the initramfs, which is on
# the `PATH` rinit gives a service, and `cut -f2` splits the tab that
# `/proc/self/status` uses between the field name and its value.
[Unit]
Description=Ambient capability demo (started on demand)

[Service]
Type=oneshot
User=nobody
Group=nogroup
NoNewPrivileges=true
CapabilityBoundingSet=NET_BIND_SERVICE
AmbientCapabilities=NET_BIND_SERVICE
ExecStart=/bin/sh -c "echo uid=$(id -u) amb=$(grep ^CapAmb /proc/self/status | cut -f2) eff=$(grep ^CapEff /proc/self/status | cut -f2) bnd=$(grep ^CapBnd /proc/self/status | cut -f2)"
